Pass over a ctor that cannot be evalled, when asked to.

`evalCtors` stops at the first ctor it cannot eval, and that is the only safe
thing it can do: what the ctor did before failing is on the instance, and
nothing rolls an instance back. An embedder with many independent ctors -- a
class initializer each, say -- has to set the failing one aside and run the
whole tool again, parsing and instantiating the module once per failure.

But the module changes only when state is applied to it, so a new instance of
the module is the state from before the failure. With `keepGoing`, a failing
ctor is evalled again on a new instance, applying as it goes so that whatever
part of it evals is kept, as it would be had evalling stopped there; then it
is passed over, named in `skipped`, and evalling goes on with the next one on
yet another instance.

Applying state is also what costs: it serializes the whole live heap. Every
application allocates a defining global per object, and their expressions stay
in the module's arena whether the globals are kept or not, so an application
per ctor costs memory in proportion to the heap times the ctors. Under
`keepGoing` the state is applied once for as many ctors as evalled since the
last failure, and after a failure those are evalled again on the new instance,
which gives the same state, being the same code from the same state. A ctor
whose export is kept is evalled as before, applying as it goes, because only
an application can serialize its result.

The step limit stays one over the whole call: the new instance carries the
count of the old, and the ctors evalled again run unbounded, being known to
finish. A ctor that runs the limit out still ends evalling, as before, since
every ctor after it would too.

Without `keepGoing` nothing is evalled differently.

On a module whose start function builds 87 000 objects, 284 ctors that all
eval took nine minutes and 31 GB in one call; under `keepGoing` they take 11 s
and 6 GB. With the 12 that fail among them, one call takes 27 s, where it took
a call per failure. The module written is the one the calls without it wrote,
up to the order of globals and which edge of a cycle in the heap is broken.

